import json
from pathlib import Path
p=Path(__file__).parent;R=json.loads((p/'reviews.json').read_text())
def put(s,d,t,ts,why,st=None,sol=None,**kw):
 r={'difficulty':d,'topic':t,'tags':ts.split(','),'reason':why,**kw}
 if st:r['statement']=st
 if sol:r['solution']=sol;r['solutionKind']='original-exposition'
 R[s]=r
put('esp_2023_1312df',4.5,'Number Theory','sum of two squares,divisibility,rational squares','A two-square identity converts the integer quotient into a rational square root between successive squares, forcing equality.',r'''Find all positive integer quadruples $(a,b,c,d)$ such that $a^2+b^2=c^2+d^2$ and $ac+bd$ divides $a^2+b^2$.''',r'''Set $a^2+b^2=k(ac+bd)$ for a positive integer $k$. The identity
$$(ac+bd)^2+(ad-bc)^2=(a^2+b^2)(c^2+d^2)$$
gives $((ad-bc)/(ac+bd))^2=k^2-1$. A rational number whose square is an integer must itself be an integer, so $k^2-1$ is a square. This forces $k=1$, since for $k\ge2$ it lies strictly between $(k-1)^2$ and $k^2$. Thus $ad=bc$, and equality of the two sums of squares forces $(a,b)=(c,d)$. All quadruples $(a,b,a,b)$ work.''')
put('esp_2023_8985e3',4,'Combinatorics','parity invariant,cube,light-switching','The invariant is the parity of the seven central lights; the possible target also needs an explicit sequence of presses.',r'''A $3\times3\times3$ cube consists of 27 unit cubes, each containing an initially unlit bulb. Pressing any unit cube except the central one toggles its bulb and the bulbs in all face-adjacent cubes. Decide whether one can obtain (a) all bulbs lit; (b) all except the central bulb lit; (c) only the central bulb lit.''',r'''Consider the seven bulbs consisting of the cube's centre and the six face-centre cubes. Every permitted press toggles either zero or two of these seven: a face-centre press toggles itself and the centre; an edge-centre press toggles two face centres; a corner press toggles none. Their lit-count parity stays even. Targets (a) and (c) have an odd count, so are impossible.

For (b), press all twelve edge-centre cubes, then all six face-centre cubes. Each corner is toggled three times; each edge centre once plus twice; each face centre four times plus once; and the centre six times. Thus precisely the central bulb remains off.''')
put('esp_2023_c70629',4.5,'Geometry','orthocentre,angle bisector,midpoint','The midpoint of the constructed hypotenuse is the useful auxiliary centre; doubling an angle shows it lies at the midpoint of AH.',r'''In an acute scalene triangle $ABC$, let $I,H$ be the incentre and orthocentre, and let $M$ be the midpoint of $AB$. Choose $D,E$ on the line $AH$ so that $MD\parallel CI$ and $ME\perp CI$. Prove $AE=DH$.''',r'''Let $N$ be the midpoint of $DE$. Since $MD\perp ME$, $N$ is the circumcentre of the right triangle $DME$. The angle between $DE$ and $EM$ equals the angle between their perpendiculars $BC$ and $CI$, namely half of $\angle BCA$. The corresponding central angle between $ND$ and $NM$ therefore equals $\angle BCA$. The angle between $AH$ and $BH$ also equals $\angle BCA$, so $MN\parallel BH$ (interpreting angles as directed angles if an intersection is on an extension). In triangle $ABH$, the line through midpoint $M$ parallel to $BH$ meets $AH$ at its midpoint. Hence $N$ is the midpoint of both $AH$ and $DE$. Reflection in $N$ exchanges $A,H$ and $D,E$, giving $AE=HD$.''')
put('esp_2023_d0a2c0',3.5,'Algebra','quadratic composition,symmetry,Vieta formulas','The axis of symmetry of the inner quadratic pairs the four real roots; constructing two quadratics from the paired roots is then immediate.')
put('esp_2023_d7aa40',6.5,'Combinatorics','token moves,sharp counting,pigeonhole principle','Summing sharp crossing-cost bounds forces equality at every boundary; a second pigeonhole argument then tracks one token far enough backwards.',r'''A row has 203 cells. Initially all 203 tokens occupy the leftmost cell. One move transfers either one token or exactly twenty tokens from one cell to an adjacent cell. After 2023 moves every cell contains one token. Prove that some token moved left at least nine times.''',r'''For the boundary with exactly $n$ cells to its right, the net number of tokens crossing rightward is $n$. Write $n=20q+r$, $0\le r<20$. If the net numbers of twenty-token and single-token crossings are $u,v$, then $20u+v=n$, and the number of moves is at least $|u|+|v|$. Its minimum is
$$q+\min(r,21-r).$$
Summing for $n=1,\ldots,202$ gives 2023: over $n=0,\ldots,199$ the total is $20(0+\cdots+9)+10\cdot109=1990$, and the last three values contribute $10+11+12=33$. Thus every boundary attains its minimum.

In particular the boundary with eleven cells to its right is crossed in exactly ten moves: once by twenty tokens rightwards and nine times by individual tokens leftwards. Consider those twenty tokens just after their shared rightward move. They all occupy the eleventh cell counted from the right. Since their final cells are distinct, one finishes at least as far left as the twentieth cell counted from the right. That token must subsequently make at least nine leftward steps, as required.''')
put('esp_2023_aabb71',6.5,'Geometry','excentre,power of a point,similar triangles','Recognising the reflected incentre as the excentre permits two power-of-a-point calculations; a separate bisector-ratio similarity supplies the final parallelism.',r'''In scalene triangle $ABC$, the angle bisector $AI$ meets the circumcircle again at $D$, where $I$ is the incentre. Reflect $I$ in $D$ to obtain $J$. Let $E,F$ lie on line $BC$ with $IE,JF\perp AI$. Let $G,H$ be the perpendicular feet of $I,J$ on $AE,AF$, respectively. Prove $BG=CH$.''',r'''The arc-midpoint lemma gives $DB=DC=DI=DJ$, and identifies $J$ as the $A$-excentre. Thus the circle $BICJ$ has diameter $IJ$, with tangents $IE,JF$. Taking powers of $E$ and $F$, and using the right triangles $AIE,AJF$, gives
$$EB\cdot EC=EI^2=EA\cdot EG,\qquad FB\cdot FC=FJ^2=FA\cdot FH.$$
Consequently $G,H$ lie on the circumcircle of $ABC$.

Put $K=AI\cap BC$. The internal and external angle-bisector theorems in triangle $ABK$ give $IA/IK=JA/JK$. Similarity of $KIE$ and $KJF$ then gives $IA/JA=IE/JF$. The right triangles $AIE$ and $AJF$ are therefore similar, with their altitude feet $G,H$ corresponding. Hence $AG/AE=AH/AF$, so $GH\parallel EF\parallel BC$. The cyclic quadrilateral $BCHG$ is an isosceles trapezium, yielding $BG=CH$.''')
put('srb_2020_235775',5.5,'Algebra','polynomials,complex roots,extremal root','Choosing a root with minimum real part makes every shifted root-distance at least one; equality collapses all roots, and multiplicity excludes higher degree.',r'''Find all monic polynomials $P$ for which $P(x+1)$ divides $P(x)^2-1$.''',r'''The solutions are $P=1$ and $P(x)=x-c$, with $c$ any constant in the coefficient field. Both satisfy the condition.

For a nonconstant monic polynomial, choose a complex root $c$ with least real part, and list all roots with multiplicities as $c,r_2,\ldots,r_n$. Divisibility gives $P(c-1)=\pm1$. But $\Re(c-1-r_i)\le-1$, hence $|c-1-r_i|\ge1$; the product of these moduli is 1, so equality holds for every $i$. Equality forces $r_i=c$. Thus $P=(x-c)^n$. Now $P(x)^2-1=(x-c)^{2n}-1$ has a simple root at $x=c-1$, whereas $P(x+1)$ has multiplicity $n$ there. Therefore $n=1$.''',section='Day 1')
put('srb_2020_7bb1fa',6.5,'Combinatorics','polyhedra,construction,planar graphs','An even face gives an easy alternating construction, but the all-odd case requires finding two suitably disjoint adjacent faces and checking a planar separation argument.',r'''A convex polyhedron has at least five vertices, with exactly three edges incident to every vertex. Prove that rational labels can be assigned to the vertices so that at least one label is 2020 and the product of the labels around every face is 1.''',section='Day 1',solution='The source gives a worked construction, with diagrams for the necessary pair of disjoint faces. Open the linked solution to follow the two cases.',solutionKind='source-link')
