put('srb_2020_1afb4f',6.5,'Geometry','radical axes,Pappus theorem,concurrency','A power identity first creates an auxiliary cyclic quadrilateral; Pappus then links its opposite-side intersection to the required radical axis.',r'''In trapezium $ABCD$ with $AB\parallel CD$ and no right interior angles, diagonals meet at $E$. Let $P,Q$ be the perpendicular feet from $A,B$ to the lines $BC,AD$, respectively. The circles $(CEQ)$ and $(DEP)$ meet again at $F$. Prove that $AP,BQ,EF$ are concurrent or parallel.''',section='Day 2',solution='The worked source uses an auxiliary cyclic quadrilateral, the radical axis, and Pappus’s theorem. The separate parallel-side case is also included.',solutionKind='source-link')
put('srb_2020_4b3837',7,'Number Theory','2-adic valuation,functional equations,residue permutations','Preservation of valuations first implies injectivity on every residue system; the bound f(x)≤3x then forces a specifically chosen residue representative to hit 3a exactly.',r'''For a positive integer $n$, let $v_2(n)$ be the exponent of 2 in its prime factorisation. Suppose $f:\mathbb N\to\mathbb N$ satisfies $f(x)\le3x$ and
$$v_2(f(x)+f(y))=v_2(x+y)$$
for all positive integers $x,y$. Prove that every positive multiple of 3 is attained by $f$ exactly once.''',r'''Taking $x=y$ gives $v_2(f(x))=v_2(x)$. Also, if $x\not\equiv y\pmod{2^k}$, choose positive $z\equiv-x\pmod{2^k}$. Then $f(x)+f(z)$ is divisible by $2^k$, whereas $f(y)+f(z)$ is not. Hence $f(x)\not\equiv f(y)\pmod{2^k}$. In particular $f$ is injective.

First let $a$ be odd, and choose $k$ with $2^{k-1}<3a<2^k$. The odd inputs below $2^k$ have all distinct odd output residues modulo $2^k$, so some odd $x<2^k$ has $f(x)\equiv3a\pmod{2^k}$. If $f(x)\ne3a$, then $f(x)>2^k$. The valuation condition gives
$$f(x)+f(2^k-x)\equiv2^k\pmod{2^{k+1}},$$
so this positive sum is at least $3\cdot2^k$. The upper bounds make it at most $3\cdot2^k$, forcing equality and $f(x)=3x$. Thus $3x\equiv3a\pmod{2^k}$, and $x=a$, contradicting $f(x)\ne3a$. Therefore $f(x)=3a$.

For general $a=2^tu$ with $u$ odd, apply this argument to $g(x)=f(2^tx)/2^t$, which is integer-valued and satisfies the same two conditions. This proves existence, while the previously established injectivity proves uniqueness.''',section='Day 2')
